{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfnEjyDM6zS5AUtFbwPBd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All three amended rows change meaning (`VERDICT: MEANING rows=3`). Two of them are blessable, but none could be re-anchored this time, because LLR-277 goes back for a rewrite.\n\n**What changed in each row**\n- **LLR-277:** two new functions enter the requirement. `tier_carriers` picks the file formats each tier may use, and `tier_rows_from_text` reads a tier from TOML, CSV or legacy markdown. An implementation correct under the old text didn't need either.\n- **LLR-262:** two new cases at the merge-time mint. If the claim can't be read, the mint is refused with the reason and the merge still stands. If there is no claim, intake writes a stderr line saying the check did not run, and mints nothing.\n- **LLR-278:** a registry is now identified by the file the kit resolves for it (TOML, legacy CSV or legacy markdown needs). An implementation that only matched the TOML path fails this for CSV or markdown registries.\n\n**Which rows I'd bless**\n- **LLR-262:** yes. The rewrite fixes everything I sent back last round: who acts, where the message goes, and what happens to the merge. It matches `intake.py`.\n- **LLR-278:** yes. It reads cleanly and names one place where a registry's file is resolved.\n- **LLR-277:** no. The new sentence was inserted just before \"`baseline_snapshot._tier_rows` reads every NEED_TIERS tier through it\". That \"it\" now reads as `tier_rows_from_text` (or the markdown needs carrier). The code actually goes through `load_need_tier` for the need tiers and `load` for the rest, so a literal reading tells a builder to rewire the snapshot comparison. I drafted the wording fix (behaviour unchanged) under a new `## Dispositions` section in `docs/work/active/wi-841/WI-841-done-when-blessed-in-lane.md`.\n\n**Re-anchoring:** I ran `intake.py snapshot --reattests LLR-262,LLR-278` to confirm it would be refused. It was, naming LLR-277's drifted Detail, and it wrote nothing. All three rows share the LLR registry's snapshot copy, so LLR-262 and LLR-278 stay unanchored. They should be re-attested together with LLR-277 in one act, once LLR-277 is reworded and re-judged.\n\n**Commits (registry cells untouched):**\n- `9f261dfa` — the verdict file `004-ADJUDICATE-9c22f9c.md`, with the trailer `WI: WI-841`.\n- `479dd7ae` — the Dispositions draft, also with `WI: WI-841`.\n\nThe pre-commit checks passed on both commits.\n\n**For the coordinator:** as last round, the owner's 2026-10-06 ruling says a returned row is fixed inside this lane. The LLR-277 fix is probably a one-sentence reword in the lane, followed by one combined re-attestation of all three rows."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1252,"cache_read_input_tokens":84052,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1252},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2cffbbc6-0175-4e90-b9ab-7b165e0c0246","uuid":"0c3c9189-3295-4dfa-9192-1b73fa1fe096","timestamp":"2026-10-07T04:22:32.218Z","request_id":"req_011CfnEjxyiiLb9Q7UsHNy1N"}
